## Summary

With `gating` enabled, a top-level function declaration that is referenced before its declaration takes the hoisted-wrapper path in `insertAdditionalFunctionDeclaration` (`Entrypoint/Gating.ts`). On that path:

- the original declaration is renamed to `Foo_unoptimized` in place;
- a new `function Foo` wrapper is inserted next to it.

If the original declaration is **exported**, the `export` keyword stays on the renamed node. So the module exports `Foo_unoptimized` and **no longer exports `Foo`**. Every importer of `Foo` gets `undefined`, whatever the gate returns, because nothing exports the wrapper that reads the gate.

```js
// input (@gating)
export const MemoFoo = memo(Foo);
export function Foo(props) { ... }

// output before this PR
export const MemoFoo = memo(Foo);
const gating_result = gating();
function Foo_optimized(t0) { ... }
export function Foo_unoptimized(props) { ... }   // export moved to the renamed function
function Foo(arg0) { ... }                        // public name is no longer exported
```

TypeScript overloads hit the same path. Overload signatures are `TSDeclareFunction` nodes that reference the name before the implementation, so any exported overloaded hook or component counts as referenced before its declaration:

```ts
export function useX(a: number): number;
export function useX(a: string): string;
export function useX(a: number | string) { ... }
```

Once `@babel/preset-typescript` erases the signatures, the only export left is `useX_unoptimized`. So turning on `gating` breaks every module that exports an overloaded hook or component, even while the gate returns `false`.

**Fix:** the public binding moves onto the gated wrapper.

- `export function Foo` becomes `function Foo_unoptimized` plus `export function Foo(...) { gated }`.
- `export default function Foo` becomes `function Foo_unoptimized` plus `export default function Foo(...) { gated }`.
- The gating-result `const` and `Foo_optimized` are inserted before the export statement instead of inside it.

Non-exported declarations are unchanged.

```js
// output after this PR
export const MemoFoo = memo(Foo);
const gating_result = gating();
function Foo_optimized(t0) { ... }
function Foo_unoptimized(props) { ... }
export function Foo(arg0) {
  if (gating_result) return Foo_optimized(arg0);
  else return Foo_unoptimized(arg0);
}
```

## How did you test this change?

I added three fixtures in `compiler/packages/babel-plugin-react-compiler/src/__tests__/fixtures/compiler/gating/`:

- `gating-use-before-decl-export.js`: an `export function` referenced before its declaration.
- `gating-use-before-decl-export-default.js`: the same case with `export default function`.
- `gating-overloaded-hook-export.tsx`: an exported hook with TypeScript overload signatures.

In all three `.expect.md` outputs, the wrapper exports the public name and `Foo_unoptimized` is no longer exported. All three evaluate successfully (`(kind: ok)`). Before this change, the same inputs produced `export function Foo_unoptimized` and no export of `Foo`.

